.TH VECBENCH 1
.SH NAME
vecbench \- time the vector operations a Krylov solve runs on
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B vecbench
[
.I n
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Vecbench
times the vector operations a
.IR krylov (2)
solve spends most of its time in, comparing what Limbo generates against
.IR math (2)'s
C builtins, on vectors of
.I n
elements (default 13824, which is 24\(mu24\(mu24).
.PP
It reports, in microseconds per call: a dot product written in Limbo,
the same through
.BR math\->dot ,
.BR math\->norm2 ,
an
.I axpy
written in Limbo both allocating its result and writing in place,
.BR math\->axpby ,
a bare
.B array[n]
allocation on its own, and a 7-point CSR matvec both in Limbo and
through
.BR math\->spmv .
.PP
The comparison it exists for is the two axpy rows against the bare
allocation.
The obvious explanation for these operations costing 40% of a
GPU-backed solve was Limbo heap traffic, since every one of them
allocated a fresh array.
That is wrong: the in-place version costs the same as the allocating
one, and a bare allocation is under a microsecond.
The cost is the generated code, which is why a C builtin helps by
several times over and removing allocations does not help at all.
.PP
The JIT is on by default and matters enormously here \(em the same
loops run about fourteen times slower under
.B -c0
\(em so comparing the two is part of what this is for.
.PP
The matvec is included because it is where the time actually goes: at
this length it costs about 360 microseconds in Limbo against 12 for all
the vector operations put together.
Its check is a worst relative difference rather than an equality, and
the matrix is deliberately built from values that are not exactly
representable.
An earlier version used 1.0 and 6.0, and reported the two loops as
identical to the bit \(em which proved nothing, because the compiler
contracts the multiply and add into a fused one and that only rounds
differently when the values need rounding at all.
.SH EXAMPLES
Compare compiled against interpreted:
.IP
.EX
vecbench
emu -c0 -r . /dis/vecbench.dis
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/vecbench.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR gpubench (1),
.IR math-linalg (2),
.IR krylov (2)
.SH BUGS
The Limbo versions are copies of what
.IR krylov (2)
used to contain, kept here for the comparison.
They will not track it if it changes again.
.PP
One length, one element type, one machine.
Nothing here says anything about a vector too large for cache, where
the balance between generated code and memory bandwidth is different.
